rt consensus at Safe-to-Likely R and markets near 92% Republican, all point the same direction. A modest shift toward the ratings consensus supports a margin around eleven points Republican.", "watch": "A major national Democratic wave environment or an unexpected scandal could narrow the margin, though flipping the seat would require an unusually large swing.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Addison McDowell (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican Addison McDowell, elected in 2024, seeks a second term in a North Carolina district that leans Republican by about nine points. He has been renominated without difficulty and has out-raised Democratic nominee Cyril Jefferson, the High Point mayor, by roughly six to one, while also holding a large cash-on-hand advantage. Jefferson won his primary in March but has raised little and entered the fall with under $15,000 on hand.\n\nNo public general-election polling is available, so the picture rests on fundamentals: the district lean, incumbency, money, and expert judgment. Every major rater places the race in the Safe, Solid, or Likely Republican column, and prediction markets price McDowell's chances above ninety percent. Barring an extraordinary shift in the national environment, McDowell is strongly favored to hold the seat.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0525, "margin": -10.38}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0423, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0544, "margin": -11.0}]}